the cost of living crisis is getting  worse and it's very likely you've  noticed it in your own life but just how  long is it going to go on for and how  bad are things going to get  there are lots of different reasons that  things are getting more expensive  there's problems in the supply chains  hangovers from the covered 19 pandemic  and of course the war in ukraine but  rising bills now means that one in four  people have said they've skipped meals  and three and five have opted to turn  off their heating in order to save money  on the bills it's energy prices that  really underpin a lot of this cost of  living crisis  [Music]  in april the energy price cap rose by a  record 54  it means average household bills are now  around 2 000 pounds a year but things  are likely to get much worse when the  cap is reviewed again in october the  latest estimates are that bills could  rise to 2  600 pounds a year possibly even more  they're then expected to stay up at  around 2 300 pounds all the way through  to the start of 2024.  energy prices are one factor that  determine the overall rate of inflation  but rising costs of food goods and fuel  all contribute as well inflation has now  hit a new forty year high of nine  percent and forecasts expect that that  will rise to over ten percent by later  this year it means the steepest price  rises for four decades  and it'll be felt in all areas of life  one key concern is how wages just aren't  keeping up with that rate of inflation  in fact in the three months to march  wages fell in real terms by 1.2 percent  compared to the same period last year  that figure is a nine-year low a real  worst case scenario is if all this led  to what's called a wage price spiral  it's where employers raise wages for  workers to try and keep up with rising  costs while businesses put up the cost  of their products to match the rising  wages each pushing the other up into an  uncontrolled spiral and very high  inflation this happened in the 1970s and  it took years of deep economic pain to  bring inflation back down  another more likely worst case scenario  is a recession that's the official term  for when an economy shrinks if a country  is producing and selling fewer goods and  services if people struggling under the  cost of living crisis simply can't  afford to spend their money in the  economy then it will shrink the bank of  england has forecasted contraction later  this year council disposal income is  projected to fall by one and three  quarter percent in this 2022 which  apart from 2011 would be the largest  contraction since comparable records  began  in 1964. that would be seriously bad  news because a shrinking economy would  likely mean pay cuts job losses and a  whole lot more pain  [Music]  the uk is far from alone with these  concerns inflation and slow economic  growth are affecting lots of countries  around the world but there are places  like japan and canada that are faring  better making economic predictions at a  time like this is always a precarious  business but unfortunately things are  likely to get worse before they get  better and much of this pain will be  with us for at least a year  [Music]
 

